          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Bouded Linear Logic (BLL) is a refinement of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            (LL) where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derelictions"} derelictions
            are annotated with a natural number indicating the number of times the associated resource can be used.
          %p
            This way, well-typed functions do not only satisfy the basic input/output specification (as in the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply-typed+lambda+calculus"} simply-typed lambda calculus
          %p
            %strong Theorem.
            A function from dyadic integers to dyadic integers is computable in polynomial time in the length of its input if and only if it is typable in BLL.
          %p
            The expressive power of BLL thus lies between that of
            %span.newWikiWord
              rudimentary linear logic
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/rudimentary+linear+logic"} ?
            (RLL)—which is sequent calculus without the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
            and without the
            = succeed "\u2014and" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction rule
          %h2#related_logical_systems Related logical systems
          %p Ugo dal Lago and Martin Hofmann have proposed a variant of BLL called QBAL, which allows for quantification over resource variables and is also sound and complete for polynomial time.
          %p
            When the derelictions are annotated with an element of a(n ordered)
            = succeed "\u00A0" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiring"} semiring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+linear+logic"} graded linear logic
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} G
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} LL
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{G}_\mathcal{S}\mathrm{LL}
            ).
